{"type": "thinking", "text": "Back to portrait, grid restored.\n\nLet me examine whether the Profile Skills grid level numbers are all present in accessibility text. They weren't (only names). But visually fine.\n\nNow, what about the main Skills overview (bottom nav Skills)? The changed \"Skills overview grid\" could be Profile grid, which we verified. Maybe there's a bug where the Profile grid level for a skill is read from the wrong source — e.g., Mercantile 99 correct, Smithing 19 correct. All matched. So no.\n\nWait, maybe the bug involves the skill *XP set* via debug: the tile shows a level not updating because it reads from a stale snapshot. The debug \"Set XP\" actually opens item count, so can't test.\n\nHowever, there might be another debug entry: Settings maybe has \"debug\" menu with set XP? The Profile grid's [Debug] Set XP is broken (opens item count). That itself could be the bug to report in the skills overview area: Each skills grid tile has a \"[Debug] Set XP\" button, but tapping it opens \"Debug: Modify Item Count\" with Item ID \"slayer\", which is clearly wrong. But is that part of the release app? It's labeled Debug, and the task's areas include skills overview. The precondition mentions specific XP values, suggesting we might use these debug buttons to set XP! The task says \"Do not change data outside what testing requires\" — maybe they expect you NOT to use debug. Hmm, \"Debug\" buttons in a QA build are normal for test harness; a broken debug tool might not be a release bug.\n\nBut the debug dialog IS weird: Item ID \"slayer\" for a skill tile. Maybe it's intentional: the \"Set XP\" button opens a generic debug item count dialog with the item id pre-filled from the skill name? No, that doesn't set XP. This could be a decoy, or the build's debug tools are intentionally miswired. I don't think we should report debug functionality.\n\nLet me look at the main Skills screen again for anything inconsistent: \"Skills (1)\" tab label. Maybe \"(1)\" indicates a notification badge for something. Could be \"1\" unallocated skill point? The Attack tree showed 0 points available and Hitpoints 3. Hmm, \"Skills (1)\" could mean 1 level-up available? no.\n\nMaybe tapping the \"Skills (1)\" tab vs \"Expeditions\" and back is fine.\n\nCould the \"Skills (1)\" be a badge count of prestige points? Unclear, not a clear bug.\n\nLet's check the \"Expeditions\" tab maybe related? Not in scope.\n\nNow let's consider the short-format change more concretely. Maybe the shop header displays 2.6M but the actual coin count is 2,567,890. If we buy the Basic Staff for 375, coins become 2,567,515 → still 2.6M. If we buy Logs with quantity e.g., 100 (700 coins) still 2.6M. Can't cross a display boundary downward except big purchase.\n\nCould sell enough to cross upward to 3.0M? Need +432,110 coins. Inventory sell total (excluding equipped, with all gear) ~5,416 + junk 486 + materials (copper 160 + logs 180 + tin 100 + stone 72 + pumpkin 372 + tomato 162 + cowhide 84 + feather 360 + wool 42) ≈ 7,434. Not near.\n\nSo no boundary test possible without huge purchase. Spending 1.9M on XP boost crosses down. 2,567,890 - 1,900,000 = 667,890. Then header should show \"667,890\" (if sub-million uses comma). If it shows \"0.7M\" that's a format choice. What bug could we detect? If it shows \"6.7M\" that's a bug, but unlikely. If it rounds 667,890 to \"667.9K\" maybe okay. If it shows \"0\" or crashes, bug. The change only affects millions; testing 667,890 confirms no regression for sub-million. Could be worthwhile to detect a bug like displaying \"668K\" when spec says comma. But unspecified.\n\nMaybe the seeded bug is that the header rounds *up* to 2.6M, letting the user think they have 2.6M; buying the 2.6M-priced item? No such item. Hmm.\n\nAnother thought: the XP Boost price \"1.9M coins\" and original \"2.5M coins\" — 25% off, but the displayed 1.9M rounded up from 1.875M. The short format should perhaps truncate (1.8M) to avoid overcharging display. Not a functional bug.\n\nCould buy the 2× XP Boost: it will charge some amount. If charged 1,900,000 (displayed) vs actual 1,875,000? Can't see exact coins. Header after purchase 667,890 suggests actual 1.9M charged (2,567,890-1,900,000=667,890). If it charged 1,875,000 → 692,890. Header would still show 667,890 vs 692,890 both as \"667,890\"/\"692,890\"? The header shows comma format presumably, so we could distinguish! That would test both short-format price and transaction. Interesting but big spend.\n\nRisk: buying XP boost (limit one/week) and spending 1.9M. It doesn't affect our areas except coins/header. Skills XP unchanged until training. Could be acceptable testing of the Shop special. But then our coin balance drops below 1M, and header format below million can be verified. We can't undo. The precondition coin count no longer holds, but we're near end of testing. If we find a bug in the transaction, great. If not, we still sign off. But QA ethics: avoid unnecessary destructive changes. I'd say testing the header's million-format boundary is relevant to the changed feature. Yet 1.9M is the only way.\n\nAlternatively, is there a cheaper item priced in millions? Necklaces 2.3M (more), nothing else. So XP boost is the cheapest million item.\n\nHmm, maybe we can sell items to get coins back? Not to 2.5M. The XP boost itself cannot be resold.\n\nGiven uncertainty, I might skip; the short format at 2.6M appears correct.\n\nLet me look at \"Recent bulk sales\" maybe it logs previous sales from save (could exist already despite empty queue?). Tap it. It may show \"No recent bulk sales\". Not changed, skip.\n\nLet's inspect Sell tab sorting by Quantity and Price to ensure cap prices consistent and no duplicate/wrong rows; quick sanity. Also test search input with empty etc. Not changed, but shop area. Let's do a quick sort test:\n- Tap \"Quantity\" → order should be Copper 40, Logs 30, Tin 25, Cowhide 14, Stone 12, etc. descending.\n- Tap \"Price\" → de