ncation/rounding to 1 decimal loses 25k.\n\nThis is a good bug to report. But wait—can we report it without having \"observed\" the exact price? The observed_quote \"1.9M coins\" (dialog) and header \"692,890\". Steps: starting coins 2,567,890 (precondition), buy XP boost displayed 1.9M; after purchase header shows 692,890, meaning 1,875,000 was deducted, not 1.9M. Expected: displayed/charged amounts agree (show exact 1,875,000 or don't round up). Symptom: wrong_value.\n\nHowever, is \"1.9M\" perhaps an acceptable rounded display of 1,875,000? The bug is more that the *checkout total* is rounded, not that the header is. The changed feature explicitly introduced short format for \"large gold amounts\" in the shop header, but the item price/dialog likely used the same formatter. The dev may say \"short format is approximate by design.\" But for a purchase price, approximating is bad; and it rounds up (user expects to pay 1.9M but pays 1.875M) — charging less isn't harmful, so severity low. Hmm.\n\nCould there be a case where rounding up harms? If a price is 2,250,000 (Necklace of Anguish, 25% off 3M), it displays \"2.3M\". A player with 2,260,000 coins sees \"2.3M\" and might think they can't afford it, though they can. Or a player with 2,300,000 would buy and be charged 2.25M. The displayed price overstates. Not harmful financially but misleading.\n\nWhat if an item's exact price is 2,349,999 and displays 2.3M (rounding down) but charges 2,349,999; a player with 2.3M would think they can afford but can't. The \"Total cost\" display could understate! For XP boost it rounds up; for some item it might round down. That's a real risk. The short format likely rounds, not truncates. 1.875→1.9 (up); 2.25→2.3 (up). All current million prices round up, it seems. If an item price were 1.94M, it'd show 1.9M (down) and charge 1.94M > displayed. Do any items have such prices? XP boost 1.875→1.9; necklace 2.25→2.3; none show down. Hmm.\n\nThe more robust bug: **prices in the purcha