. (loss). But we can test \"Sell Old Gear\" with toggle ON: sells Bronze Axe ×1 and Iron Sword ×2 (stacks). That would lose 2 Iron Swords and 1 Bronze Axe permanently, and unequip? Iron Sword is equipped; selling the extra 2 leaves one? With toggle ON, keeps one, so equipped sword remains? It sells 2 of 3 inventory copies; inventory count goes from 3 to 1, still equipped. Data change significant but not catastrophic. Could instead do \"Sell Junk\" with toggle ON: loses 13 cowhide, 59 feathers, 6 wool (junk), keeps one each. Reversible only by grinding. Hmm.\n\nBut testing the actual bulk sale could reveal a bug: e.g., \"Keep one\" is applied in the preview but not on actual sale (would sell all), or the total paid differs. With individual sale we verified toggle enforcement (snackbar). With bulk, we haven't executed. The seeded bug might be that the toggle is **not respected when actually selling** (only preview). That's a plausible bug! The changed feature is persistence, though—not enforcement. And we saw individual enforcement works.\n\nMaybe the seeded bug is that the **toggle resets to default OFF after a bulk sale** (state not saved). Could test with a small bulk sale and then reopen. But data loss.\n\nAlternatively, use \"Sell Junk\" with toggle ON and then check the preview; we did. Actual sale should follow the preview. QA best practice would execute once. The materials lost are low-value and the task allows data changes for testing. But precondition inventory would be violated (cowhide 14→1, etc.). Would that matter? We've already spent 1.9M coins; precondition coins violated. We're deep in. The app state after tests isn't expected to remain pristine, presumably. The instruction \"Do not change data outside what testing requires\" allows changes that testing requires. Testing bulk sale enforcement requires actually selling. We could sell the junk stack (cowhide/feather/wool) with toggle ON, verify each item keeps 1, then toggle OFF and verify? Reversal isn't possible (can't restore hiides), but the sale is a legitimate test.\n\nActually, we can test the actual bulk sale while keeping one of each, and then verify inventory counts = 1 for those three items. That would confirm enforcement. If instead they hit 0, that's a bug (toggle preview lied). That's worth testing! Let's do it. The loss is ~468 coins worth of junk; we gain coins. The precondition inventory changes (cowhide 14→1, feather 60→1, wool 7→1). Hmm, that's a lot of materials, but they're low-tier junk. QA testing often consumes test data. The task says use the starting data; it doesn't demand restoring. We already altered coins drastically. I think it's acceptable.\n\nBut before that, maybe we can test actual bulk sale with a smaller, more controlled stack: \"Sell Old Gear\" with toggle ON sells Bronze Axe ×1, Iron Sword ×2. Those are equipment; losing them may affect gear/combat tests. Junk is safer.\n\nLet's do Sell Junk with toggle ON and confirm:\n- Preview: 13 cowhide (78), 59 feather (354), 6 wool (36), total 468.\n- Tap Sell All.\n- Check snackbar and inventory counts (cowhide 1, feather 1, wool 1).\n- Check toggle stays ON and persists after reopening.\n\nThis tests bulk enforcement and possibly the toggle save after a transaction. Then we can set toggle OFF (restore default) and maybe the inventory remains minimal—fine.\n\nWait, if we sell junk with ON, the \"Recent bulk sales\" will record a bulk sale of 468. We can then check that section for correctness/persistence—nice extra coverage.\n\nLet's do it. Currently Buy tab with toggle? We're on Buy, toggle state in Sell is OFF. Need to go Sell, toggle ON, Sell Junk. Then Sell All.\n\nTap Sell [1], toggle [7], Sell Junk [4]."}
