{"type": "thinking", "text": "Back on Skills. The Cooking row \"⏰\" and \"..\" is just emoji/quest; screenshot clarified.\n\nNow, the \"Skills (1)\" badge: Let's investigate because it might be a bug in skills overview. What does the \"(1)\" mean? Maybe it's a notification count of open quests; there are two gold dots (Farming and Cooking). If the badge counts \"skills with available quests\" it should be 2. If it counts \"prestige ready\" it's 1 (Mercantile). Let's check Settings description: \"Prestige Notification Badges — Show a notification badge on navigation when a skill is ready to prestige.\" This is ON. Mercantile is level 99 and not prestiged (★×0, Prestige button) → 1 skill ready to prestige → badge \"(1)\" on Skills nav. That matches! The gold dots are quest dots (\"Quest dots on skills\" ON) for Farming and Cooking. So badge = prestige-ready count 1. Correct.\n\nGood.\n\nSo skills grid static is correct; only dynamic update untestable.\n\nMaybe we can trigger a *level-independent* shared state change: **prestige** would reset Mercantile to 0 and change the badge, but destructive. No.\n\nWhat about the **Mercantile XP display** \"13.0M XP\" uses short format. The shared state level 99. Fine.\n\nI think we can't find a skills bug without leveling; maybe none exists.\n\nNow #1 toggle: any untested aspect? \"the setting is now stored with the character's save data.\" What if we **sell all items with keep-one ON**, then **close the game**, reopen, and the toggle OFF? We tested close/reopen. Fine.\n\nWhat if we toggle ON, then **switch to a different tab and change another setting (Compact Numbers)**, then revisit—does keep-one persist? yes, independent.\n\nWhat if we toggle ON, then **buy an item** (store purchase) and reopen—persist? likely.\n\nMaybe the seeded bug is that the toggle is stored **only when you leave the Sell tab**, not immediately. If the app is killed while on Sell right after toggling, it's lost. Our exit via BACK from Sell may have triggered the save. Could test by toggling ON then pressing HOME (app backgrounded) then `open_app`—we did similar with BACK. Backgrounding triggers onPause/onStop, likely save. To test immediate kill, need force-stop.\n\nI'll consider #1 fine.\n\nMaybe there is another bug in the shop header: the confirmed bug is price rounding. The header itself shows 2.6M for 2,567,890—maybe the header is also wrong (rounds up). Could report the header separately? It's the same short-format rounding issue. We already reported the price mismatch. The developer confirmed, so they'll fix the formatter. No need.\n\nWait, note the header *after* purchase showed **\"692,890\"** (exact with comma) rather than a short \"692.9K\". So the new short format applies only ≥1M, and under 1M uses exact. Good.\n\nNow, maybe the payout cap bug has more instances. We found copper/tin. What about **Stone** sell 6 vs current buy 6 (equal) — not a profit, but the cap spec might require strict less? No.\n\nWhat about **Logs** sell 6 vs base 10 — okay.\n\nWhat about items with payouts above their *current* catalogue but we didn't test due to not being sold? Only inventory items. We covered all sell rows vs catalogue where possible.\n\nCould there be a shop item in inventory that we overlooked? Let's compare precondition inventory to sell list:\nPrecondition: Bronze Pickaxe x1, Bronze Axe x2, Bronze Fishing Rod x1, Bronze Boots x1, Iron Sword x3, Bronze Dagger x1, Agility Cape x1, Mithril Platebody, Adamantite Platebody, Slayer Platebody, Runite Chainbody, Runite Platebody, Gilded Platebody x1 each, Steel Full Helmet x1, Mithril Med Helmet x1, Copper Ore x40, Tin Ore x25, Stone x12, Logs x30, Pumpkin x12, Tomato x9, Cowhide x14, Feather x60, Wool x7.\nSell list (initial): Bronze Dagger, Iron Sword, Adamantite Platebody, Agility Cape, Bronze Boots, Gilded Platebody, Mercantile Cape (extra, from Mercantile 99), Mithril Med Helmet, Mithril Platebody, Runite Chainbody, Runite Platebody, Slayer Platebody, Steel Full Helmet, Bronze Axe, Bronze Fishing Rod, Bronze Pickaxe, Copper Ore, Logs, Tin Ore, Cowhide, Feather, Pumpkin, Stone, Tomato, Wool.\nAll present. Mercantile Cape extra is expected from level 99. Good.\n\nPayouts vs current prices only for shop items: copper, tin, logs, stone. Done.\n\nSo #3 fully covered.\n\nNow, maybe there's a bug in the **Sell tab cap for the *equipped* items**: e.g., Iron Sword payout 62, but its catalogue price (not in shop) maybe 250; fine.\n\nOK.\n\nLet's think about the \"Skills overview grid\" change once more. The Profile grid levels are correct at rest. Could the seeded bug be that the grid shows **XP-based level but the level circle's progress ring is missing/wrong**? The screenshot showed rings with no visible progress for most skills (grey circles). Wait, in the Profile grid, the level circles are grey rings; only Smithing/Mercantile have gold rings. If the ring is supposed to show XP progress, then most are not showing progress. But maybe grey circles are just level badges, and gold indicates a quest/prestige.\n\nLook at the first Profile screenshot: Mining circle \"50\" had a grey ring, and the ring is just a circle, not a progress arc. Smithing's ring was gold and had a gap at top (looks like 98% progress). Mercantile gold full. In the second screenshot, Crafting ring grey, etc. If the ring were level progress, Mining (90%) should show a mostly complete arc; it didn't. But why is Smithing gold? Because Smithing has an open quest? The main Skills screen shows a gold dot next to... let's check screenshot: Farming and Cooking have gold dots; Smithing does NOT have a gold dot. Hmm! In the Skills screenshot, gold dots are next to Farming and Cooking, not Smithing. But in the Profile grid, Smithing's ring was gold. So gold ring ≠ quest dot. Maybe gold ring = level-up ready? Smithing is 70 XP from level 20 — nearly level up, but not \"ready\". Mercantile 99 ready to prestige. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe gold ring = \"level 19 is close to 20\"? The ring could show XP progress as a gold arc: Smithing 4,400/4,470 = 98.4% → mostly gold. Mercantile 99 at max → full gold. Mining 101,333/111,945 = 90.5% → should be 90% gold, but screenshot ring looked grey? Let me look more carefully at the first Profile screenshot (7:32): Mining circle has a grey ring, but maybe the arc is a *dark blue* that blends; the level \"50\" is in a circle with grey outline. Smithing's ring clearly had a gold arc from bottom around most of the circle. Mining's should too. Maybe the screenshot compression hid a blue/grey arc? Actually the ring color might depend on the skill category (gathering blue, crafting gold?) — no, Crafting 20 was grey.\n\nHmm, maybe the ring is a **quest/prestige indicator** and Smithing/Mercantile have something (Smithing has an open quest? The main screen didn't show a gold dot for Smithing, but it showed a small \"!\" or timer icon next to Cooking; Smithing had a plain row with gold progress bar). The Smithing row's progress bar itself was gold (not blue) in the main Skills screenshot! Yes! In the Skills screenshot, Smithing's horizontal progress bar was **gold**, while Mining/Fishing/Woodcutting/Crafting bars were blue. Why? Because Smithing has an XP boost active? Wait, we bought a 2× XP boost after that screenshot (the screenshot was taken at 7:45, after purchase). But this recent screenshot: Smithing bar gold, Cooking bar blue. The 2× XP boost is active globally, so all XP should double? Hmm, maybe the gold bar indicates \"level up imminent\" or \"selected\".\n\nActually, in the initial screenshot (before XP boost purchase), Smithing's bar in the Skills list was **gold** too! Look at the first Skills screenshot at 7:26: Smithing row had a gold bar, while others blue. So gold bar = something specific to Smithing. Maybe a gold bar indicates a \"quest boost\" or \"bonus XP\" for that skill. The \"Skills (1)\" badge? Hmm.\n\nWhat is special about Smithing? It has 4,400/4,470 XP, 0.85915494 fraction (86%? Actually 4400/4470 = 98.4%; the \"0.85915494\" might be something else). Wait, the row's 4th item \"0.85915494\" could be the progress fraction of the *current session*? 4400/4470=0.984; 0.859 is not that. Hmm, maybe 0.859 is the \"prestige XP multiplier\"? For other rows 0.0. Smithing has a gold ba", "truncated": true}
